WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.500
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.402 --> 00:00:07.860
ARI LIBERMAN: With
me here today is

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:10.190
Adrian Graham, Cloud
Solutions Architect

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:11.660
for Media and Entertainment.

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:12.860
My name is Ari Liberman.

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:15.320
I'm a product manager
for Google Compute Engine

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:16.640
and for cloud GPUs.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:19.364
So let's get right into it.

00:00:19.364 --> 00:00:21.725
Thanks, Adrian.

00:00:21.725 --> 00:00:23.610
So we have a few things
to talk about today.

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:25.151
This is an introductory
session, so I

00:00:25.151 --> 00:00:27.680
want to talk about how far
we've come in the past year

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:30.527
since we introduced the
first generation cloud GPU.

00:00:30.527 --> 00:00:33.110
We're going to be talking about
some exciting announcements we

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:35.984
have for you around virtual
workstations on GCP.

00:00:35.984 --> 00:00:37.400
And then I'm going
to hand it over

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.566
to Adrian, who's going to
be speaking about building

00:00:39.566 --> 00:00:41.510
a visual effects
studio in the cloud,

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:42.770
and show you with a demo.

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:49.540
So last year, around
February of 2017,

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:53.170
we introduced our first
generation cloud GPU, the K80.

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:55.070
It's an NVIDIA Tesla GPU.

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:56.770
We've come a long
way since then.

00:00:56.770 --> 00:00:59.080
But I'm just going to give
you a quick refresher on why

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:01.660
cloud GPUs.

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:04.489
Computation and graphics
intensive applications

00:01:04.489 --> 00:01:07.870
have need for massively
parallel computation.

00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:10.120
This means that a lot of the
processing that you could

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:14.350
do in CPU is not enough to do
some jobs like high performance

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:17.470
computing and machine learning.

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:23.060
When you compare and
contrast a GPU to a CPU,

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:28.600
CPUs are great for
many jobs, for just

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:30.730
like your typical operations.

00:01:30.730 --> 00:01:33.250
They can perform a lot of jobs.

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:36.910
But the GPU can do a lot
more parallel processing.

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:39.250
For example, a CPU
can have in the order

00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:42.105
of 20 to 40 cores per CPU.

00:01:42.105 --> 00:01:43.480
And if you have
multiple sockets,

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:46.120
you can scale up to
160 cores, like we

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:49.540
have in the recently
announced [INAUDIBLE] machine.

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:53.330
But on a GPU you can have
in the order of 5,000

00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:55.960
or even more stream processors.

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:58.150
So you can perform a lot
of parallel computation

00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:00.980
very quickly.

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:04.880
The other advantage of GPUs is
that it has much higher memory

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:05.380
bandwidth.

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:07.570
So you can be moving
data across very quickly,

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:11.110
compared to a CPU where
that might be slow.

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:12.820
And that's why in
the past few years

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:15.749
we've seen a significant
uptake for many applications.

00:02:15.749 --> 00:02:18.040
And especially you've seen
today a lot of announcements

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:19.870
on machine learning,
where there's

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:24.280
been a crazy demand for
accelerators in the cloud,

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:27.340
for things like machine
learning training and machine

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:29.470
learning inference.

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:31.150
And then we have
also known that GPUs

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:32.800
are very popular
for applications

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:38.720
like simulation, genomics,
and, of course, visualization.

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:41.060
So one of our objectives
in Google Cloud Platform

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:42.950
is to give you one
place where you

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:44.900
can accelerate all
of your workloads,

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:46.500
regardless of what that is.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:50.610
So you might have a need
for Intel Skylake CPUs.

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:54.170
We introduced Skylake before
any other cloud provider, back

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:55.820
last year.

00:02:55.820 --> 00:03:00.470
We have a broad
range of NVIDIA GPUs.

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:03.320
And you saw today some more
information on cloud GPUs,

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:07.720
which are accelerators for
machine learning, training,

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:11.460
optimized for TensorFlow.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:13.770
So one of the new additions
today to this family

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:16.420
is that we have a
graphics-optimized offering.

00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:18.420
And I'll be talking more
about that in a second.

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:22.890
So why Google Cloud?

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:25.889
And I think you've seen
this over the years.

00:03:25.889 --> 00:03:27.680
So we have one of the
best infrastructures.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:31.410
We build fiberoptics thousands
of miles under the ocean

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:32.580
and over land.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.910
And you have very
scalable performance

00:03:34.910 --> 00:03:37.890
and reliable performance.

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:39.690
We've also been
working for many years

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:42.130
in optimizing our VMs
and our boot times.

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:44.430
So you can create up
to 4,000 instances

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:45.390
in less than a minute.

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:48.070
That's something you
cannot do anywhere else.

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:49.890
This is all with our
security built in.

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:52.260
So we've learned from
building services like Gmail--

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:54.750
you saw today [? Robert Carr ?]
talking about Gmail

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:56.260
and how far we've come there.

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:59.370
So from building services that
have over a billion users,

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:02.550
we take some of those lessons
and we apply them GCE.

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:04.860
And also you got the benefits
of per-second billing.

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:08.130
So you only pay for the
resources for the exact time

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:11.240
that you use them, not
for hours or any longer.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:18.660
So how does cloud GPU work?

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:21.479
The way it works is when
you create an instance,

00:04:21.479 --> 00:04:23.520
if you're familiar with
our custom machine types,

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:26.157
you can create a machine with
as many cores as you need,

00:04:26.157 --> 00:04:27.740
however much memory
you need, and then

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:30.986
you can attach anywhere
from one up to eight GPUs.

00:04:30.986 --> 00:04:32.360
So you have a lot
of flexibility.

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:34.735
And this is something that
you cannot find anywhere else.

00:04:34.735 --> 00:04:36.960
This means that if your
application is very memory

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:39.000
bound, you can add more memory.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.520
If you need a lot
more GPUs to do things

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:43.500
like machine learning,
you can attach them,

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:46.180
and you can get your
job done much faster.

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:47.790
But if you don't
need any of them,

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:50.800
you can optimize and
save a lot of money.

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:53.100
For example, if you're
doing a simulation that

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:55.890
requires very few
cores, some memory,

00:04:55.890 --> 00:04:57.510
if we're scaling
up to 1,000 nodes,

00:04:57.510 --> 00:05:00.210
imagine if you multiply that
times the amount of memory

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:01.149
for each VM.

00:05:01.149 --> 00:05:03.190
You end up saving a lot
of money in the long run.

00:05:03.190 --> 00:05:05.370
So we give you that
flexibility so you only

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:06.710
pay for what you use.

00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:09.180
And it's by the second.

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:10.770
Some of the other
cloud GPU features

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:13.510
is that you get the GPU
in pass-through mode.

00:05:13.510 --> 00:05:15.960
This means that you get
the bare metal performance.

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:18.180
We don't virtualize the device.

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:19.980
I talked about custom
machines, and that's

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:21.810
a significant benefit.

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:23.580
I talked about
per-second billing.

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:25.260
But I think, most
importantly, you get

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:26.640
a broad range of frameworks.

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:29.010
For example, within
the [INAUDIBLE]

00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:30.660
you have [INAUDIBLE].

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:33.790
We have tested TensorFlow
and other frameworks.

00:05:33.790 --> 00:05:35.242
So there's a very
broad ecosystem,

00:05:35.242 --> 00:05:37.200
and you could take
advantage of that regardless

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:39.460
of your application, if
it's simulation or machine

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:42.630
learning or visualization.

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:45.230
So today we have three
families of GPUs--

00:05:45.230 --> 00:05:48.180
the K80, the P100 that we
introduced in September

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:51.840
of last year, and the V100.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:54.780
There's different combinations
depending on the accelerator.

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:58.560
For example, with the V100 you
can attach up to four GPUs.

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:00.340
With Volta, our
latest generation,

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:02.880
which was introduced
a few months ago,

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:04.380
you can attach up to eight.

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:06.754
One of the benefits of the
latest generation of the Volta

00:06:06.754 --> 00:06:09.810
GPU is that you have NVLink,
which is a high speed

00:06:09.810 --> 00:06:11.490
connectivity across the GPUs.

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:14.700
So your application could take
advantage of a bigger memory,

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:18.775
and much faster computation.

00:06:18.775 --> 00:06:20.400
These are some of
the ranges also here.

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:22.970
You can see on how many
GPUs and how many cores,

00:06:22.970 --> 00:06:25.080
and you have a lot
of flexibility.

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:28.570
You can customize your instance.

00:06:28.570 --> 00:06:30.990
So I'm going to talk a little
bit about workstations now.

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:34.270
And I have some
announcement for you today.

00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:36.690
So first, if you're
in some industries,

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:39.330
like if you have a graphics
workstation, like media

00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:41.340
and entertainment,
or oil and gas,

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:43.380
you typically could go
buy a [INAUDIBLE] card

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:46.200
you [INAUDIBLE] under your
desk or in some data center.

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:49.560
And I think that there's a lot
of advances in the recent years

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:51.300
that sometimes make that stale.

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:53.310
So you have a GPU that
might be a few years old.

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:55.980
It's not easy to keep up
with all the driver updates,

00:06:55.980 --> 00:06:57.960
and to keep all the
software updates.

00:06:57.960 --> 00:06:59.910
And also some of the
application providers

00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:01.590
don't give consistent
support across all

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:02.850
the different devices.

00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:03.792
It's getting better.

00:07:03.792 --> 00:07:05.250
And we're hearing
from our partners

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:08.350
that there's more consistency,
but it's still a challenge.

00:07:08.350 --> 00:07:10.680
One of the other problems is
that you might have silos.

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:13.080
You're working,
let's say, in LA,

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:16.560
and you're trying to connect to
somebody in Montreal or London.

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:18.270
And moving data is very slow.

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:21.037
It's difficult.
And it's also, you

00:07:21.037 --> 00:07:22.620
don't know if there's
enough security.

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:24.720
So you need to make sure
that all of your data

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:25.824
is safe at all times.

00:07:25.824 --> 00:07:27.240
And one of the
other challenges is

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:30.120
that if, for example, you're
rendering a large movie,

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:33.210
you want to make sure that you
have access to other services.

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:36.250
So when you press the button
to start doing the rendering,

00:07:36.250 --> 00:07:38.770
that's over in close proximity.

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:41.260
And you can do this much faster.

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:44.040
So today we're going
to change this.

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:46.410
And we're introducing a
new type of accelerator

00:07:46.410 --> 00:07:47.660
on Google Compute Engine.

00:07:47.660 --> 00:07:50.310
We're introducing
the NVIDIA Tesla P4.

00:07:50.310 --> 00:07:55.770
The P4 is a graphics
optimized GPU from NVIDIA.

00:07:55.770 --> 00:07:58.325
But it's not only
a graphics GPU.

00:07:58.325 --> 00:08:00.450
I'm going to talk about
some of the specs on the P4

00:08:00.450 --> 00:08:01.700
and why it's different.

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:04.980
With a P4 you get 8
gigabytes of frame buffer.

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:07.350
So you have 8
gigabytes of memory

00:08:07.350 --> 00:08:09.300
for your graphics
intensive application.

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:14.070
We have close to 200 gigabytes
per second of memory bandwidth.

00:08:14.070 --> 00:08:15.750
But it's not only
good for graphics.

00:08:15.750 --> 00:08:18.130
It's a card that is
great for compute.

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:21.750
So if you're doing things like
machine learning inference,

00:08:21.750 --> 00:08:25.560
to give you an example, or if
you're doing video transcoding,

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:27.870
it's a great fit for
those use cases, too.

00:08:27.870 --> 00:08:29.620
It also has the coding
and encoding agent.

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:32.789
So it's great for
reader transcoding.

00:08:32.789 --> 00:08:37.480
With P4 we're also introducing
NVIDIA GRID support.

00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:40.230
This means that you get
a [INAUDIBLE] workstation

00:08:40.230 --> 00:08:43.919
in the cloud with multiple
monitoring support, 4K

00:08:43.919 --> 00:08:46.590
resolution, with the latest
generation of workstation

00:08:46.590 --> 00:08:47.665
clouds GPU.

00:08:47.665 --> 00:08:51.270
This includes support for Pascal
features and deep learning.

00:08:51.270 --> 00:08:54.240
And this is up to 1.3x the
performance of previous

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:56.407
generation Tesla GPUs.

00:08:56.407 --> 00:08:57.990
What this means in
the end is that you

00:08:57.990 --> 00:09:00.090
can take on the biggest
challenges, things

00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:01.860
like photorealistic
environments,

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:03.920
immersive applications.

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:05.670
And as I mentioned
before, you get

00:09:05.670 --> 00:09:08.974
the GPU and pass-through mode,
so you get the most performance

00:09:08.974 --> 00:09:09.640
out of your GPU.

00:09:12.150 --> 00:09:14.630
The way you attach a virtual
workstation to an instance

00:09:14.630 --> 00:09:17.330
is the same as
you do with a GPU.

00:09:17.330 --> 00:09:20.060
You select the accelerator type,
in this case, the NVIDIA Tesla

00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:20.896
P4.

00:09:20.896 --> 00:09:21.770
And it's very simple.

00:09:21.770 --> 00:09:24.260
You just append -vws.

00:09:24.260 --> 00:09:27.200
That converts the Tesla P4
GPU, which is a compute card,

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:28.582
into a virtual workstation.

00:09:28.582 --> 00:09:30.290
You can select anywhere
from one to four.

00:09:30.290 --> 00:09:32.750
And in this case, I'm
creating a custom machine type

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:37.330
with 64 cores and 300
gigabytes of memory.

00:09:37.330 --> 00:09:39.050
But we didn't stop there.

00:09:39.050 --> 00:09:42.530
We're also introducing
support for P100.

00:09:42.530 --> 00:09:45.129
So if you need more
memory or more power

00:09:45.129 --> 00:09:47.420
for your application, let's
say you're doing simulation

00:09:47.420 --> 00:09:49.089
with something like
Discovery Live,

00:09:49.089 --> 00:09:50.630
you need to be able
to take advantage

00:09:50.630 --> 00:09:53.390
of more of the simulation
capabilities of your GPU.

00:09:53.390 --> 00:09:56.000
So you can turn a P100,
with GRID support,

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:59.510
into a virtual workstation
if you need that much power.

00:09:59.510 --> 00:10:01.250
One of our customers,
Schlumberger,

00:10:01.250 --> 00:10:03.450
has been using cloud
GPUs since last year.

00:10:03.450 --> 00:10:07.190
And Schlumberger, they have very
demanding needs for simulation,

00:10:07.190 --> 00:10:08.930
but also for visualization.

00:10:08.930 --> 00:10:10.520
They've deployed
virtual workstations

00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:13.490
in several regions, and their
oil and gas subject matter

00:10:13.490 --> 00:10:16.830
experts can power
their Petrotechnical

00:10:16.830 --> 00:10:20.390
Suite in the DELFI cognitive
and E&amp;P environment.

00:10:20.390 --> 00:10:23.760
And they can power devices
all over the globe.

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:25.760
They take advantage of
our low-latency network

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:28.940
and also our global
availability of GPUs

00:10:28.940 --> 00:10:31.160
to allow their users to make
more informed decisions,

00:10:31.160 --> 00:10:34.695
from oil and gas exploration,
all the way to production.

00:10:34.695 --> 00:10:36.820
So it's been very good for
them since we introduced

00:10:36.820 --> 00:10:39.210
this in alpha a few months ago.

00:10:39.210 --> 00:10:40.690
Here's a pricing on P4.

00:10:40.690 --> 00:10:46.690
The P4 GPU starts at
$0.60 an hour per GPU.

00:10:46.690 --> 00:10:48.750
You can attach one,
two, or four GPUs.

00:10:48.750 --> 00:10:52.180
And it's the same memory
and core configurations

00:10:52.180 --> 00:10:54.920
as a P100 GPU.

00:10:54.920 --> 00:10:56.780
But there's one addition--

00:10:56.780 --> 00:10:57.900
you need a license.

00:10:57.900 --> 00:11:01.772
So there's $0.20 an hour
per license per hour.

00:11:01.772 --> 00:11:03.730
If you need more power,
as I mentioned earlier,

00:11:03.730 --> 00:11:05.750
you can also use a P100 GPU.

00:11:05.750 --> 00:11:10.790
And the P100 GPU
starts a $1.46 an hour.

00:11:10.790 --> 00:11:12.860
And you can also add
the [INAUDIBLE] license

00:11:12.860 --> 00:11:15.220
to that to come up
with a total cost.

00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:18.590
P4 GPUs and P100 GPUs also
are supported and preemptible,

00:11:18.590 --> 00:11:21.140
so they're allowed for
less than 24 hours.

00:11:21.140 --> 00:11:22.790
And they can be
preempted at any time.

00:11:22.790 --> 00:11:27.734
So this is also supported
with a P4 and P100.

00:11:27.734 --> 00:11:29.150
One of our partners
and customers,

00:11:29.150 --> 00:11:32.920
BeBop Technology, they've been
using a variety of our NVIDIA

00:11:32.920 --> 00:11:34.774
GPUs for virtual workstations.

00:11:34.774 --> 00:11:37.190
And they've had great success
with media and entertainment

00:11:37.190 --> 00:11:38.087
customers.

00:11:40.710 --> 00:11:43.600
And P4 is going to be rolling
out over the next few weeks.

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:45.780
We're going to have them
available in locations

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:48.391
like Iowa, in northern
Virginia, and Netherlands.

00:11:48.391 --> 00:11:50.640
And I think for our media
and entertainment customers,

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:53.160
they're going to be available
in LA as well as Montreal

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:54.330
over the coming weeks.

00:11:54.330 --> 00:11:56.340
We have more regions
launching sometime

00:11:56.340 --> 00:11:58.690
in Q4 in the end of this year.

00:11:58.690 --> 00:12:00.150
So stay tuned,
since they're going

00:12:00.150 --> 00:12:05.560
to be rolling out throughout
this year in more locations.

00:12:05.560 --> 00:12:08.681
But your application
and your GPU, it's

00:12:08.681 --> 00:12:10.430
not only about the
hardware that you have.

00:12:10.430 --> 00:12:14.440
You also need a great client to
be able to experience the best

00:12:14.440 --> 00:12:15.760
graphics application.

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:18.670
So we've partnered with Teradici
to provide Cloud Access Plus

00:12:18.670 --> 00:12:21.970
Software through the
Google Cloud Marketplace.

00:12:21.970 --> 00:12:24.320
You can go in,
create an instance,

00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:27.580
and then use the fairly
cheap PC over IP protocol.

00:12:27.580 --> 00:12:29.400
This means that you
get color accurate

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:32.879
and lossless video quality.

00:12:32.879 --> 00:12:34.420
So I think it's a
great addition that

00:12:34.420 --> 00:12:38.210
will make a lot of our
customers very successful.

00:12:38.210 --> 00:12:43.090
The CEO of Teradici, they've
been testing on P4 and P100

00:12:43.090 --> 00:12:45.000
and enabling some
of their customers.

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:48.560
And basically, they've
seen a lot of success

00:12:48.560 --> 00:12:51.520
and a lot of great
feedback so far.

00:12:51.520 --> 00:12:54.070
So with that, I'm
going to hand it over

00:12:54.070 --> 00:12:55.570
to Adrian, who's
going to be talking

00:12:55.570 --> 00:12:58.620
about how to build a virtual
effects studio in the cloud.

00:12:58.620 --> 00:12:59.690
And thank you.

00:12:59.690 --> 00:13:00.765
ADRIAN GRAHAM: All right.

00:13:00.765 --> 00:13:01.390
Thank you, Ari.

00:13:04.180 --> 00:13:05.710
So a little introduction.

00:13:05.710 --> 00:13:10.180
I'm a cloud solutions architect
for media and entertainment,

00:13:10.180 --> 00:13:11.920
working in Los Angeles.

00:13:11.920 --> 00:13:14.740
And I spent, prior
to working at Google,

00:13:14.740 --> 00:13:18.920
20 years working
in visual effects.

00:13:18.920 --> 00:13:20.890
Worked at some big facilities.

00:13:20.890 --> 00:13:22.900
Worked on big films.

00:13:22.900 --> 00:13:26.300
And I mostly worked on the tech
side and the pipeline side.

00:13:26.300 --> 00:13:29.320
So basically, it was my job
to make things work for all

00:13:29.320 --> 00:13:30.340
the artists.

00:13:30.340 --> 00:13:32.590
So my job now at
Google is to figure out

00:13:32.590 --> 00:13:34.960
how to run all those same
workloads, but on the cloud.

00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:37.210
So we've been doing a lot
of really interesting stuff.

00:13:37.210 --> 00:13:39.400
And I'm going to sort of
go into a little more tech

00:13:39.400 --> 00:13:42.640
detail, some of the
stuff that Ari mentioned.

00:13:42.640 --> 00:13:45.700
First, let's have
a little primer

00:13:45.700 --> 00:13:48.850
as to what an architecture
might look like to deploy

00:13:48.850 --> 00:13:51.710
a virtual workstation.

00:13:51.710 --> 00:13:55.760
So we're going to start on
the screen left-hand side.

00:13:55.760 --> 00:13:58.370
You've got your laptops, or
what's called a zero client,

00:13:58.370 --> 00:14:00.280
which is a thin client.

00:14:00.280 --> 00:14:02.350
And you've got a gateway,
an internet gateway.

00:14:02.350 --> 00:14:04.480
And you need to
connect to the cloud.

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:06.340
Well, you can connect
over public internet.

00:14:06.340 --> 00:14:07.330
That's fine.

00:14:07.330 --> 00:14:09.250
Depending on the protocol
you use to connect,

00:14:09.250 --> 00:14:11.170
it's encrypted or not
encrypted-- hopefully

00:14:11.170 --> 00:14:12.790
encrypted.

00:14:12.790 --> 00:14:16.630
There's also connectivity
Cloud Interconnect products

00:14:16.630 --> 00:14:19.930
that we offer, like Cloud
VPN, or Dedicated Interconnect

00:14:19.930 --> 00:14:22.360
which is a 10 gigabit line.

00:14:22.360 --> 00:14:23.860
And you can have
up to eight of them

00:14:23.860 --> 00:14:25.270
right into a Google data center.

00:14:25.270 --> 00:14:28.565
And that reduces latency and
ping times, the number of hops,

00:14:28.565 --> 00:14:29.440
et cetera, et cetera.

00:14:29.440 --> 00:14:32.060
It's based on where you
are located in the world.

00:14:32.060 --> 00:14:34.930
But what we're doing is
we're connecting directly

00:14:34.930 --> 00:14:36.340
to a virtual workstation.

00:14:36.340 --> 00:14:38.680
This is the simplest
case, where we just

00:14:38.680 --> 00:14:41.380
have an IP address
of a virtual machine,

00:14:41.380 --> 00:14:43.340
and you just plug right into it.

00:14:43.340 --> 00:14:45.340
And I'm going to show
you that in a second.

00:14:45.340 --> 00:14:49.090
However, if you have more
than one virtual workstation,

00:14:49.090 --> 00:14:51.670
you can't just click a button
and deploy, deploy, deploy.

00:14:51.670 --> 00:14:53.590
What if have you have
100 artists all working?

00:14:53.590 --> 00:14:54.965
You're going to
need a connection

00:14:54.965 --> 00:14:58.540
broker or a management layer
or an orchestration layer.

00:14:58.540 --> 00:15:01.951
And that's what Ari was talking
about BeBop Technologies

00:15:01.951 --> 00:15:02.450
offers.

00:15:02.450 --> 00:15:04.660
And there's other partners
that offer that as well

00:15:04.660 --> 00:15:06.490
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:15:06.490 --> 00:15:09.050
So then we start to build
out our infrastructure.

00:15:09.050 --> 00:15:11.890
And this is the license server,
or the NVIDIA GRID license

00:15:11.890 --> 00:15:13.660
that Ari was talking about.

00:15:13.660 --> 00:15:17.180
It is completely transparent
to the user, by the way.

00:15:17.180 --> 00:15:19.432
Once you configure a
virtual workstation

00:15:19.432 --> 00:15:20.890
with a certain type
of accelerator,

00:15:20.890 --> 00:15:23.020
it automatically talks to
the GRID license server,

00:15:23.020 --> 00:15:25.000
draws a license, so
you don't have to worry

00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:26.770
about hooking up to it.

00:15:26.770 --> 00:15:29.200
This license server,
however, is what I'm using--

00:15:29.200 --> 00:15:31.540
it's just a small VM, a
cloud license server--

00:15:31.540 --> 00:15:35.320
I'm using to provide licenses
for my third party software.

00:15:35.320 --> 00:15:37.390
I'm using Maya and
Houdini, for example.

00:15:37.390 --> 00:15:38.990
I'll show you that in a second.

00:15:38.990 --> 00:15:42.850
And then there's some other
data storage platforms

00:15:42.850 --> 00:15:44.920
that you might need
some shared file system,

00:15:44.920 --> 00:15:46.520
or if you want to
use Cloud Storage.

00:15:46.520 --> 00:15:48.940
Those are all connected to
your virtual workstation, which

00:15:48.940 --> 00:15:51.100
is a virtual machine
running on GCP,

00:15:51.100 --> 00:15:56.500
just like any virtual
machine that you have.

00:15:56.500 --> 00:15:57.760
And then we can expand out.

00:15:57.760 --> 00:16:00.070
And this is getting
a little bit more

00:16:00.070 --> 00:16:02.650
into the visual effects era.

00:16:02.650 --> 00:16:04.750
And that's-- we've
got render workers,

00:16:04.750 --> 00:16:08.650
which perform offline
compute that feed data back

00:16:08.650 --> 00:16:10.180
to our virtual workstations.

00:16:10.180 --> 00:16:12.820
And then we can orchestrate
it all with a Queue Manager

00:16:12.820 --> 00:16:14.650
to distribute tasks
and everything.

00:16:14.650 --> 00:16:17.200
So this might seem
slightly familiar to you

00:16:17.200 --> 00:16:19.450
if you're not in visual
effects or animation

00:16:19.450 --> 00:16:20.826
or in the
entertainment industry,

00:16:20.826 --> 00:16:22.616
but really they're all
the same components.

00:16:22.616 --> 00:16:24.130
You have a bunch
of compute that you

00:16:24.130 --> 00:16:26.440
need to send out to
different workers

00:16:26.440 --> 00:16:31.750
and orchestrate it, get it back,
record it in database entries.

00:16:31.750 --> 00:16:34.870
But the big difference
here is that we're

00:16:34.870 --> 00:16:36.670
able to visualize it.

00:16:36.670 --> 00:16:41.320
So I'm going to take you
through a quick pretend day

00:16:41.320 --> 00:16:42.370
in the life of an artist.

00:16:42.370 --> 00:16:44.244
And there's a few people
in the audience that

00:16:44.244 --> 00:16:45.430
know what this is all about.

00:16:45.430 --> 00:16:49.870
Let's pretend that at 10:00
AM an artist rolls in,

00:16:49.870 --> 00:16:52.570
and they're reviewing their
shots or their feedback

00:16:52.570 --> 00:16:53.890
from the night before.

00:16:53.890 --> 00:16:55.870
So they only need a
modest workstation.

00:16:55.870 --> 00:16:57.620
They don't need a
massive workstation.

00:16:57.620 --> 00:17:01.300
So they can spin up a
machine with only eight CPUs

00:17:01.300 --> 00:17:03.400
and 8 gigs of RAM and
a P4, for example.

00:17:03.400 --> 00:17:06.190
That's a relatively
inexpensive workstation.

00:17:06.190 --> 00:17:09.130
And it's of decent power.

00:17:09.130 --> 00:17:11.560
But once they get
feedback and they

00:17:11.560 --> 00:17:13.810
need to start really
doing some work,

00:17:13.810 --> 00:17:17.470
like doing some fluid
simulations or crazy amounts

00:17:17.470 --> 00:17:21.130
of compute, they can reconfigure
their virtual workstation,

00:17:21.130 --> 00:17:25.060
add more CPUs, add more RAM, and
add more GPUs, multiple GPUs.

00:17:25.060 --> 00:17:26.530
And this is one of
the key elements

00:17:26.530 --> 00:17:29.680
of GCP, is that you can
attach GPUs to almost

00:17:29.680 --> 00:17:31.390
any configuration of a machine.

00:17:31.390 --> 00:17:35.090
You dial up or down how
much RAM, how many CPUs.

00:17:35.090 --> 00:17:37.420
Then you could also
add multiple GPUs.

00:17:37.420 --> 00:17:38.800
Depending on the
type of GPU, you

00:17:38.800 --> 00:17:42.430
can add one, two, or four
P4s or P100s, for example.

00:17:42.430 --> 00:17:43.960
Or if you're using
V100s, you can

00:17:43.960 --> 00:17:48.280
have up to eight V100s, which
is mind-blowingly powerful.

00:17:48.280 --> 00:17:53.050
OK, so this artist is
done with that task.

00:17:53.050 --> 00:17:56.050
But now they need to render it,
so they need even more power.

00:17:56.050 --> 00:17:59.080
But they don't need
massive amounts of CPUs,

00:17:59.080 --> 00:18:00.770
they need massive
amounts of GPUs.

00:18:00.770 --> 00:18:04.530
So say, for example,
they're doing GPU rendering.

00:18:04.530 --> 00:18:06.382
Well, they could
attach a bunch of GPUs

00:18:06.382 --> 00:18:07.840
to their local
workstation, or they

00:18:07.840 --> 00:18:12.382
can deploy a cluster of
machines with GPUs attached

00:18:12.382 --> 00:18:13.840
and address those
in a certain way.

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:15.756
I'm actually going to
show you how to do that.

00:18:15.756 --> 00:18:18.220
And this is the
game changer here,

00:18:18.220 --> 00:18:21.220
that now you've got
your own personal GPU

00:18:21.220 --> 00:18:25.870
farm, like your own cluster
that you could size up or down

00:18:25.870 --> 00:18:29.260
and use as much or as little
resources as you need.

00:18:29.260 --> 00:18:30.940
And because these
machines are all

00:18:30.940 --> 00:18:33.022
in the same region
and zone, they

00:18:33.022 --> 00:18:34.480
can talk to each
other really fast.

00:18:34.480 --> 00:18:36.680
I'll get into some
of that in a bit.

00:18:36.680 --> 00:18:40.060
All right, so let's pretend
that the artist is done at 6:00

00:18:40.060 --> 00:18:42.460
PM, which never happens.

00:18:42.460 --> 00:18:47.860
They stop their machine,
and they don't pay for it.

00:18:47.860 --> 00:18:49.894
I hopped too fast there.

00:18:49.894 --> 00:18:50.810
They don't pay for it.

00:18:50.810 --> 00:18:53.140
So these are all
rough calculations

00:18:53.140 --> 00:18:56.350
of what it would cost to run
any one of these configurations

00:18:56.350 --> 00:18:58.810
on a per-hour basis,
billed by the second.

00:18:58.810 --> 00:19:01.340
But at night when they go
home, they're not using it,

00:19:01.340 --> 00:19:03.160
so they're not paying for it.

00:19:03.160 --> 00:19:08.820
And also you're not paying for
a substantially powerful system

00:19:08.820 --> 00:19:13.367
when you're only doing, like,
email and viewing movie files

00:19:13.367 --> 00:19:15.700
and getting feedback from the
director the night before.

00:19:15.700 --> 00:19:18.340
So that's the power of cloud.

00:19:18.340 --> 00:19:21.280
That's really unique in that
you can deploy these resources

00:19:21.280 --> 00:19:25.370
as you need them and size
them exactly as you need them.

00:19:25.370 --> 00:19:28.690
So now let's move on.

00:19:28.690 --> 00:19:30.860
That was an individual
workstation setup.

00:19:30.860 --> 00:19:34.434
Now let's consider what it takes
to deploy an entire studio.

00:19:34.434 --> 00:19:36.070
And when I say
studio, it doesn't

00:19:36.070 --> 00:19:38.890
have to be a creative
content studio.

00:19:38.890 --> 00:19:40.510
This could be just
any group of users

00:19:40.510 --> 00:19:44.476
that wants to access a bunch
of virtual workstations.

00:19:44.476 --> 00:19:45.850
So the three major
things we need

00:19:45.850 --> 00:19:50.110
to talk about, latency,
bandwidth, egress.

00:19:50.110 --> 00:19:52.450
Latency is simply
how long it takes

00:19:52.450 --> 00:19:55.070
signal to go from
your entry point,

00:19:55.070 --> 00:19:59.050
whether it's a laptop like I'm
using now or a zero client,

00:19:59.050 --> 00:20:03.340
to the GPU or to the machine,
and back, round trip time.

00:20:03.340 --> 00:20:05.710
The example I give
here is how long

00:20:05.710 --> 00:20:08.710
it takes to go to our
Oregon data center.

00:20:08.710 --> 00:20:13.030
And it's about 31
milliseconds roundtrip.

00:20:13.030 --> 00:20:15.970
And of course, it depends
on your connectivity.

00:20:15.970 --> 00:20:21.429
That's actually from the wired
connection of my laptop here.

00:20:21.429 --> 00:20:23.470
If you do it over Wi-Fi,
it's going to be slower.

00:20:23.470 --> 00:20:25.600
If you do it with a
dedicated interconnect

00:20:25.600 --> 00:20:29.560
and you're in San Francisco,
it's going to be faster.

00:20:29.560 --> 00:20:33.024
And Ari was mentioning
we've deployed GPUs--

00:20:33.024 --> 00:20:35.440
we're going to be deploying
GPUs in some of our metro data

00:20:35.440 --> 00:20:35.940
centers.

00:20:35.940 --> 00:20:38.020
So Los Angeles is
coming online soon,

00:20:38.020 --> 00:20:41.020
Montreal, London, and Sydney.

00:20:41.020 --> 00:20:43.760
Those are all centers
for media centers.

00:20:43.760 --> 00:20:46.510
And when you are two
milliseconds away from a data

00:20:46.510 --> 00:20:48.760
center, you've got really
unique capabilities

00:20:48.760 --> 00:20:54.520
that allow you to work as if
the machine was under your desk.

00:20:54.520 --> 00:20:57.280
Bandwidth is a really
interesting problem.

00:20:57.280 --> 00:20:59.630
And it all depends on
what's on your screen.

00:20:59.630 --> 00:21:02.410
So if you have a
protocol like Teradici,

00:21:02.410 --> 00:21:04.750
Teradici only sends
the delta, the changing

00:21:04.750 --> 00:21:09.220
pixels every refresh,
every 60 hertz.

00:21:09.220 --> 00:21:11.800
So that means that if most
of your monitor is static--

00:21:11.800 --> 00:21:14.920
this is just an animated
GIF of me screen capturing

00:21:14.920 --> 00:21:17.120
moving around in a Maya scene--

00:21:17.120 --> 00:21:19.810
the stuff that's not changing,
that doesn't get resent.

00:21:19.810 --> 00:21:22.750
So only the stuff inside that
viewport is getting resent.

00:21:22.750 --> 00:21:27.800
So in an instance like this,
5 megabits connection is fine.

00:21:27.800 --> 00:21:30.610
But if you're viewing
maybe a full screen video,

00:21:30.610 --> 00:21:33.790
then your bandwidth
requirements are going to go up.

00:21:33.790 --> 00:21:38.570
So maybe 15 megabits,
20 megabits per second.

00:21:38.570 --> 00:21:41.710
But what if you put something
like noise on your screen,

00:21:41.710 --> 00:21:44.890
where every single pixel is
changing with every refresh?

00:21:44.890 --> 00:21:47.050
You could cap it at 50,
but it's going to use more.

00:21:47.050 --> 00:21:48.670
And you know what's
hilarious, is

00:21:48.670 --> 00:21:53.200
that the resolution of your
monitor makes a difference.

00:21:53.200 --> 00:21:54.850
How many monitors
makes a difference.

00:21:54.850 --> 00:21:57.850
What kind of a user you are--

00:21:57.850 --> 00:22:00.100
if you're like a twitchy
artist, and you're constantly

00:22:00.100 --> 00:22:02.224
moving windows around and
tumbling around the scene

00:22:02.224 --> 00:22:05.080
and everything, you're
going to use more bandwidth.

00:22:05.080 --> 00:22:07.565
And there are ways of
capping this per user.

00:22:07.565 --> 00:22:09.190
So you don't have to
worry that they're

00:22:09.190 --> 00:22:10.780
going to saturate
like a 10 gig line,

00:22:10.780 --> 00:22:12.820
like one user's
going to saturate it.

00:22:12.820 --> 00:22:16.810
So there are controls
and ways to restrict

00:22:16.810 --> 00:22:19.540
how much each user is assigned.

00:22:19.540 --> 00:22:23.500
But the way that we figure
it is that if you have

00:22:23.500 --> 00:22:26.350
an artist with dual
2K monitors, and they

00:22:26.350 --> 00:22:29.380
are an average workflow, you
can assign each artist 40,

00:22:29.380 --> 00:22:32.170
45 megabits per second
and they'll be fine.

00:22:32.170 --> 00:22:34.960
So that means that you could fit
50 artists on a single 10 gig

00:22:34.960 --> 00:22:37.360
line, which is not--

00:22:37.360 --> 00:22:41.230
that's not out of reach
of most facilities.

00:22:41.230 --> 00:22:43.240
The last piece of
the puzzle is egress.

00:22:43.240 --> 00:22:45.740
So this is something that
everybody that deals with Cloud

00:22:45.740 --> 00:22:46.240
knows.

00:22:46.240 --> 00:22:48.160
The data that you
pull out of Cloud

00:22:48.160 --> 00:22:50.740
is going to cost you money.

00:22:50.740 --> 00:22:53.069
Egress is a lot lower
with a virtual workstation

00:22:53.069 --> 00:22:53.860
than you may think.

00:22:53.860 --> 00:22:58.720
It's kind of hard to quantify
exactly what a pixel costs you.

00:22:58.720 --> 00:23:01.800
Because a pixel is not
necessarily changing--

00:23:01.800 --> 00:23:04.740
it's not either on or off.

00:23:04.740 --> 00:23:07.390
The bit depth that it
is and how it's deployed

00:23:07.390 --> 00:23:10.270
and the protocol that you send
it along makes a difference.

00:23:10.270 --> 00:23:13.690
So I keep talking about
Teradici only sending the delta.

00:23:13.690 --> 00:23:15.760
Well, that means
that you're going

00:23:15.760 --> 00:23:19.870
to pay less for data that's sent
down that's not moving as much.

00:23:19.870 --> 00:23:22.980
If an artist keeps a video
looping on their screen

00:23:22.980 --> 00:23:25.070
overnight, you're
going to pay for that.

00:23:25.070 --> 00:23:27.070
So you need to have
these controls in place

00:23:27.070 --> 00:23:29.410
to make sure that idle
resources, resources

00:23:29.410 --> 00:23:31.870
that aren't being
attended to, are not

00:23:31.870 --> 00:23:34.660
utilizing excess bandwidth.

00:23:34.660 --> 00:23:37.510
This is an actual
chart of, I think,

00:23:37.510 --> 00:23:39.914
three months of my egress usage.

00:23:39.914 --> 00:23:41.830
And I've been demoing
this stuff all the time.

00:23:41.830 --> 00:23:44.260
And it comes to
a total of $3.79.

00:23:44.260 --> 00:23:46.210
It's a lot lower
than you might think.

00:23:46.210 --> 00:23:48.460
But there are computations
and calculations

00:23:48.460 --> 00:23:51.670
that you can use to
do rough estimates.

00:23:51.670 --> 00:23:56.140
So one final thing before
we get into the demo,

00:23:56.140 --> 00:23:58.960
and that's you really
need a dedicated line.

00:23:58.960 --> 00:24:01.390
If you really want to
have a cloud studio,

00:24:01.390 --> 00:24:05.470
you need to have an isolated
line that is designated just

00:24:05.470 --> 00:24:08.710
for transmission to and from
your virtual workstations.

00:24:08.710 --> 00:24:11.020
We've had some people
run proof of concepts,

00:24:11.020 --> 00:24:14.620
and they complain
to us that, oh,

00:24:14.620 --> 00:24:16.420
it started getting really slow.

00:24:16.420 --> 00:24:17.650
I don't know what's going on.

00:24:17.650 --> 00:24:21.670
And we have a 50 gig
or 100 gig connection.

00:24:21.670 --> 00:24:23.980
Well, it turns out that
they had some massive Aspera

00:24:23.980 --> 00:24:27.130
upload going that was totally
stepping on and crushing

00:24:27.130 --> 00:24:28.342
their bandwidth.

00:24:28.342 --> 00:24:29.800
So this just goes
to show, you need

00:24:29.800 --> 00:24:34.230
to have dedicated bandwidth and
an assigned allotment per user

00:24:34.230 --> 00:24:35.560
on there.

00:24:35.560 --> 00:24:36.850
All right.

00:24:36.850 --> 00:24:39.100
So the demo that I'm going
to show you basically

00:24:39.100 --> 00:24:43.660
takes you down the path
of three steps, sort

00:24:43.660 --> 00:24:45.710
of a three-step journey.

00:24:45.710 --> 00:24:47.590
The first journey is
talking about what's

00:24:47.590 --> 00:24:50.620
only possible on
cloud in general.

00:24:50.620 --> 00:24:52.210
And the second
step is only what's

00:24:52.210 --> 00:24:54.040
possible on Google Cloud.

00:24:54.040 --> 00:24:57.520
And the third is only what's
possible on Google Cloud

00:24:57.520 --> 00:25:00.500
with virtual workstations.

00:25:00.500 --> 00:25:03.060
So I am on a laptop here.

00:25:03.060 --> 00:25:08.170
And I just switched to
the Teradici client, which

00:25:08.170 --> 00:25:10.000
is a software client,
runs on your laptop,

00:25:10.000 --> 00:25:12.170
runs on Windows, Mac.

00:25:12.170 --> 00:25:15.670
And there's also a hardware
client, which is a little box.

00:25:15.670 --> 00:25:19.230
But what you're seeing
here, this is not--

00:25:19.230 --> 00:25:20.150
there we are.

00:25:20.150 --> 00:25:22.720
So this isn't very
interesting, right?

00:25:22.720 --> 00:25:26.620
This is a 160 core,
160 vCPU machine

00:25:26.620 --> 00:25:30.220
running in Oregon that is
running six concurrent fluid

00:25:30.220 --> 00:25:32.710
simulations, using a
program called Houdini.

00:25:32.710 --> 00:25:34.700
I'll get into that in a second.

00:25:34.700 --> 00:25:37.630
But what this is doing is
this is generating literally

00:25:37.630 --> 00:25:40.750
terabytes of data every hour.

00:25:40.750 --> 00:25:44.980
And it's writing all that
data to a Cloud Filestore, 64

00:25:44.980 --> 00:25:46.360
terabyte, premium instance.

00:25:46.360 --> 00:25:48.160
Cloud Filestore--
I don't think it

00:25:48.160 --> 00:25:56.080
was announced at the keynote,
but it's our managed shared NFS

00:25:56.080 --> 00:25:57.400
file system now.

00:25:57.400 --> 00:25:59.830
So up to 64 terabytes.

00:25:59.830 --> 00:26:04.150
And it is manageable as an NFS
filer on Windows and Linux.

00:26:04.150 --> 00:26:06.430
But the amazing part here
is that, so this data

00:26:06.430 --> 00:26:10.190
is being written from
this one massive machine.

00:26:10.190 --> 00:26:12.800
So what can I do
with all this data?

00:26:12.800 --> 00:26:15.440
Well, I can visualize it
on a virtual workstation.

00:26:15.440 --> 00:26:20.340
So first I'm going to
show you this data is--

00:26:20.340 --> 00:26:22.350
when you do a screen
capture of it,

00:26:22.350 --> 00:26:23.650
it looks something like this.

00:26:23.650 --> 00:26:25.450
I'm running six
of these right now

00:26:25.450 --> 00:26:27.305
on that virtual workstation.

00:26:27.305 --> 00:26:28.180
This is pre-rendered.

00:26:28.180 --> 00:26:30.500
This is not running
live, that you see.

00:26:30.500 --> 00:26:33.160
But I wanted to give you an
idea of why this is useful.

00:26:33.160 --> 00:26:35.350
These are what we call wedges.

00:26:35.350 --> 00:26:38.062
When you do a simulation for--

00:26:38.062 --> 00:26:39.520
it doesn't have to
be a simulation.

00:26:39.520 --> 00:26:43.517
It could also be an
animation or any type of--

00:26:43.517 --> 00:26:45.600
seem to be getting a little
bit of-- there we are.

00:26:48.240 --> 00:26:51.354
It's really hard to tweak
all the parameters at once.

00:26:51.354 --> 00:26:52.770
So what they do
is they'll come up

00:26:52.770 --> 00:26:53.978
with something that looks OK.

00:26:56.400 --> 00:27:01.170
And then they'll
deploy six or 10 or 20

00:27:01.170 --> 00:27:04.200
variations that have one tiny
little parameter shifted.

00:27:04.200 --> 00:27:06.990
And then you can render out
each one, and you could view it

00:27:06.990 --> 00:27:09.330
and you could say,
OK, well wedge one

00:27:09.330 --> 00:27:10.890
has the parameter set at 0.5.

00:27:10.890 --> 00:27:13.950
And wedge two has the
parameter set at 0.75,

00:27:13.950 --> 00:27:14.940
or something like that.

00:27:14.940 --> 00:27:17.065
And you'll be able to see,
oh my god, just changing

00:27:17.065 --> 00:27:21.250
that one little parameter gives
me this massive difference.

00:27:21.250 --> 00:27:24.380
This is the playback
of the movie file.

00:27:24.380 --> 00:27:27.870
That's why it's a little choppy.

00:27:27.870 --> 00:27:31.590
But the idea here is that I've
got that massive machine that's

00:27:31.590 --> 00:27:33.570
able to write out all
of these simulations,

00:27:33.570 --> 00:27:35.502
run them all at the same time.

00:27:35.502 --> 00:27:41.130
And we're able to-- let
me just quit out of here.

00:27:41.130 --> 00:27:43.020
And we're able to
now visualize them.

00:27:43.020 --> 00:27:47.140
So this is a program
called Houdini.

00:27:47.140 --> 00:27:51.210
Let me just get a little
room for my mouse here.

00:27:51.210 --> 00:27:54.880
This data, I'm able to read
live off of that Filestore.

00:27:54.880 --> 00:27:57.779
This is a different machine.

00:27:57.779 --> 00:27:59.070
This is my virtual workstation.

00:27:59.070 --> 00:28:01.830
I've been viewing all that
on my virtual workstation

00:28:01.830 --> 00:28:04.110
that you've been looking at.

00:28:04.110 --> 00:28:05.880
So I'm able to read
all that data live.

00:28:05.880 --> 00:28:08.040
And we're talking
hundreds of millions

00:28:08.040 --> 00:28:13.804
of particles, gigabytes of
data that I'm able to see--

00:28:13.804 --> 00:28:14.970
my mouse keeps sliding down.

00:28:14.970 --> 00:28:17.340
I'm just going to put
this guy down there.

00:28:17.340 --> 00:28:19.980
I'm able to visualize this
stuff and read it right off

00:28:19.980 --> 00:28:21.930
that Cloud Filestore,
as it's being

00:28:21.930 --> 00:28:26.730
written by that massive
simulation engine.

00:28:26.730 --> 00:28:31.080
So this is really
important, because now I'm

00:28:31.080 --> 00:28:32.640
generating all the
data on the cloud,

00:28:32.640 --> 00:28:34.560
and I'm leaving it up
there on the cloud.

00:28:34.560 --> 00:28:36.780
So I don't have to download
these terabytes of data

00:28:36.780 --> 00:28:40.410
to my on-prem
workstation to do this.

00:28:40.410 --> 00:28:42.660
And I can access
this from anywhere.

00:28:42.660 --> 00:28:43.620
I'm on a laptop here.

00:28:43.620 --> 00:28:46.314
I can take my laptop and
bring it on an airplane,

00:28:46.314 --> 00:28:47.730
and with Wi-Fi on
an airplane I've

00:28:47.730 --> 00:28:49.950
actually connected to
these workstations before.

00:28:49.950 --> 00:28:53.430
It's not very-- you've
got 1 megabit per second.

00:28:53.430 --> 00:28:56.850
Teradici's very adaptive
to different bit rates.

00:28:56.850 --> 00:29:00.690
All right, so we've got this
simulation that we've chosen.

00:29:00.690 --> 00:29:02.070
And we like it.

00:29:02.070 --> 00:29:04.830
So now what can we
do with it next?

00:29:04.830 --> 00:29:07.486
I'm going to move over to an
application called Maya now.

00:29:07.486 --> 00:29:09.610
And again, this is all
running on the same machine.

00:29:09.610 --> 00:29:14.010
This is a standard Linux virtual
workstation running in Oregon,

00:29:14.010 --> 00:29:15.450
running CentOS 7.

00:29:15.450 --> 00:29:18.640
We can do Windows and Linux.

00:29:18.640 --> 00:29:20.790
So this is an
application called Maya.

00:29:20.790 --> 00:29:26.712
And this is a meshed
version of that simulation.

00:29:26.712 --> 00:29:28.170
Actually, let me
show you what that

00:29:28.170 --> 00:29:29.461
looks like with this turned on.

00:29:29.461 --> 00:29:30.310
There we are.

00:29:30.310 --> 00:29:35.600
So once the simulation is
done with the particle-based

00:29:35.600 --> 00:29:38.250
simulation, you can put it
through a process by which you

00:29:38.250 --> 00:29:39.270
actually surface it.

00:29:39.270 --> 00:29:40.170
You mesh it.

00:29:40.170 --> 00:29:41.700
And this is the resulting mesh.

00:29:41.700 --> 00:29:45.300
But this mesh is in a file
format called Alembic.

00:29:45.300 --> 00:29:46.680
And these files are massive.

00:29:46.680 --> 00:29:48.990
This is, I think,
a 6 gigabyte file.

00:29:48.990 --> 00:29:50.370
And I can scrub through it.

00:29:50.370 --> 00:29:53.640
This is, again, all stored
on the Cloud Filestore

00:29:53.640 --> 00:29:55.990
that I was talking
about earlier.

00:29:55.990 --> 00:30:00.150
So I'm able to scrub around, and
now I can actually go and turn

00:30:00.150 --> 00:30:00.790
on rendering.

00:30:00.790 --> 00:30:05.370
So once we have the scene
simulated and meshed,

00:30:05.370 --> 00:30:07.020
now we want to
look at how to make

00:30:07.020 --> 00:30:10.980
it appear as if it's water, a
character coming out of water.

00:30:10.980 --> 00:30:13.380
And there's a lot more
that I could talk about,

00:30:13.380 --> 00:30:15.360
but I'm running out of time.

00:30:15.360 --> 00:30:17.130
But if you want
to see more, we're

00:30:17.130 --> 00:30:20.780
doing this in a cloud studio
across the street and upstairs.

00:30:20.780 --> 00:30:22.510
I'll talk about
that in a second.

00:30:22.510 --> 00:30:27.120
So I'm going to go into--

00:30:27.120 --> 00:30:29.940
we're using a
renderer called V-Ray.

00:30:29.940 --> 00:30:32.310
And V-Ray is by a company
called Chaos Group.

00:30:32.310 --> 00:30:36.360
And V-Ray takes
advantage of GPUs.

00:30:36.360 --> 00:30:38.880
It has the ability to
render on CPUs or GPUs.

00:30:38.880 --> 00:30:43.990
But because GPUs can
run parallel processes

00:30:43.990 --> 00:30:46.770
of a way, way higher
core count, they

00:30:46.770 --> 00:30:49.390
can render a lot
faster than on CPUs.

00:30:49.390 --> 00:30:50.850
So here I've got--

00:30:50.850 --> 00:30:54.330
on this workstation
it's a single P100.

00:30:54.330 --> 00:30:57.270
And I'm able to tumble around
and look at this scene.

00:30:57.270 --> 00:30:59.890
And I can change the
lighting if I want.

00:30:59.890 --> 00:31:02.280
I can go and increase the--

00:31:02.280 --> 00:31:05.580
where did that setting go--
the intensity [INAUDIBLE]..

00:31:05.580 --> 00:31:08.100
So I can tweak my scene and
make my shaders look the way

00:31:08.100 --> 00:31:09.960
that I need them to,
and make the surfaces

00:31:09.960 --> 00:31:11.550
look the way I need them to.

00:31:11.550 --> 00:31:14.920
But you'll see this is
a progressive renderer.

00:31:14.920 --> 00:31:17.700
So as I tumble around in
real time, it'll update.

00:31:17.700 --> 00:31:20.220
And it'll sort of
iteratively update

00:31:20.220 --> 00:31:22.830
until it achieves a certain
what we call shading rate.

00:31:22.830 --> 00:31:23.550
So that's great.

00:31:23.550 --> 00:31:25.380
This is fast.

00:31:25.380 --> 00:31:27.900
But it could be even faster.

00:31:27.900 --> 00:31:31.210
And I'm limited to the number
of GPUs I have on this machine.

00:31:31.210 --> 00:31:32.380
But this is the cloud.

00:31:32.380 --> 00:31:36.260
So I'm going to deploy an entire
cluster of GPUs to render this.

00:31:36.260 --> 00:31:39.750
This cluster will be external
to this virtual workstation.

00:31:39.750 --> 00:31:41.856
They will be-- remember
that diagram earlier

00:31:41.856 --> 00:31:43.480
where I showed the
virtual workstation,

00:31:43.480 --> 00:31:45.840
and then below it was a
whole cluster of GPUs?

00:31:45.840 --> 00:31:47.590
Well, I'm going to
turn that on now.

00:31:47.590 --> 00:31:50.081
So I'm going to turn
off rendering for now.

00:31:50.081 --> 00:31:51.580
I'm going to go
into the Option box.

00:31:51.580 --> 00:31:52.645
And I will turn on--

00:31:55.770 --> 00:31:57.560
let me just quit out of--

00:31:57.560 --> 00:31:59.480
I'm going to go to
the Settings tab.

00:31:59.480 --> 00:32:02.300
And I'm going to turn on
distributed rendering.

00:32:02.300 --> 00:32:05.950
I seem to be a little bit--

00:32:05.950 --> 00:32:08.190
think my connectivity
is-- there we go.

00:32:08.190 --> 00:32:09.474
OK.

00:32:09.474 --> 00:32:10.680
Ah, all right.

00:32:10.680 --> 00:32:13.270
I'm going to turn on Use
Distributed Rendering.

00:32:13.270 --> 00:32:18.060
So I have a bunch
of virtual machines

00:32:18.060 --> 00:32:20.850
that are running V-Ray
in sort of server mode.

00:32:20.850 --> 00:32:23.220
And they're all located at
these internal IP addresses.

00:32:23.220 --> 00:32:26.130
So these virtual machines
are in the same project

00:32:26.130 --> 00:32:27.960
as my virtual workstation.

00:32:27.960 --> 00:32:30.600
And you address them
over a particular port.

00:32:30.600 --> 00:32:32.550
It's a feature of V-Ray.

00:32:32.550 --> 00:32:36.330
So now I'm going to
turn on rendering again.

00:32:36.330 --> 00:32:40.275
And if I look at the shell
that I launched Maya in,

00:32:40.275 --> 00:32:42.150
you could see, it's
starting the V-Ray server

00:32:42.150 --> 00:32:46.210
on all of those machines
at a particular frame.

00:32:46.210 --> 00:32:50.280
So if we give it a second or
two, we let them all catch up.

00:32:50.280 --> 00:32:53.820
So now when I tumble, it's
able to resolve way faster.

00:32:58.270 --> 00:33:02.330
You can actually see Teradici
adapting to the changing bit

00:33:02.330 --> 00:33:02.830
rate.

00:33:02.830 --> 00:33:06.200
Because I think my connection
speed dropped for a second

00:33:06.200 --> 00:33:06.700
there.

00:33:06.700 --> 00:33:08.500
But it adapted,
and now it's back.

00:33:08.500 --> 00:33:10.882
So it's resolving a lot faster.

00:33:10.882 --> 00:33:12.340
And this is something
that's really

00:33:12.340 --> 00:33:15.010
only possible on Google Cloud.

00:33:15.010 --> 00:33:17.560
Because the speed with
which my virtual workstation

00:33:17.560 --> 00:33:20.080
is talking to these
render servers

00:33:20.080 --> 00:33:22.150
is 32 gigabits per second.

00:33:22.150 --> 00:33:24.100
That's actually a
newly announced--

00:33:24.100 --> 00:33:27.299
we announced that a couple of
weeks ago at a launch event.

00:33:27.299 --> 00:33:28.840
It used to be 16
gigabits per second.

00:33:28.840 --> 00:33:32.050
But now these machines are able
to communicate with each other

00:33:32.050 --> 00:33:33.430
at 32 gigabits per second.

00:33:36.760 --> 00:33:43.000
So the last thing I wanted to
talk about is our cloud studio.

00:33:43.000 --> 00:33:46.810
So if you go across the
street to Moscone West

00:33:46.810 --> 00:33:49.990
and go upstairs, we
are running a live--

00:33:49.990 --> 00:33:51.160
and this is not a demo--

00:33:51.160 --> 00:33:53.830
we're running a live
virtual cloud studio

00:33:53.830 --> 00:33:56.560
with five artists working
on virtual workstations.

00:33:56.560 --> 00:33:57.890
Four of them are here.

00:33:57.890 --> 00:33:59.269
One of them is in Los Angeles.

00:33:59.269 --> 00:34:01.060
And they're all working
on a similar set-up

00:34:01.060 --> 00:34:01.810
that you saw here.

00:34:01.810 --> 00:34:03.610
They're using
virtual workstations,

00:34:03.610 --> 00:34:05.980
writing to Cloud Filestore,
running using BeBop,

00:34:05.980 --> 00:34:09.969
orchestrating with
BeBop, using NVIDIA GPUs,

00:34:09.969 --> 00:34:13.000
viewing it with Teradici, and
a whole other bunch of partner

00:34:13.000 --> 00:34:14.870
technologies and
internal technology.

00:34:14.870 --> 00:34:17.656
So I urge everybody, if
you have more questions,

00:34:17.656 --> 00:34:19.239
go across the street,
come talk to us.

00:34:19.239 --> 00:34:22.030
We're there throughout
the show, because we

00:34:22.030 --> 00:34:26.860
are building a 30-second
commercial for GCP

00:34:26.860 --> 00:34:28.150
during the show.

00:34:28.150 --> 00:34:31.060
So you'll see the animators
actually working and running

00:34:31.060 --> 00:34:33.310
things like Maya and
rendering with V-Ray

00:34:33.310 --> 00:34:34.510
and rendering using Zinc.

00:34:34.510 --> 00:34:37.389
I could talk to you
about that there.

00:34:37.389 --> 00:34:39.250
They will be delivering
a finished piece

00:34:39.250 --> 00:34:43.490
by Thursday afternoon, which
will go live on our website.

00:34:43.490 --> 00:34:48.340
[MUSIC PLAYING]

